/// \defgroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets Gadget helpers: tab control, combo box, buttons, progress bar, text entry
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief Free functions that create, lay out, recolour and drive the
///        native window gadgets: the tab control, the combo box, check-like
///        push buttons, the progress bar and the text entry field.
///
/// A gadget is a GameWindow with a gadget style bit (push button, combo box,
/// entry field, progress bar, tab control and so on) and a gadget-specific
/// block of user data hanging off the window. The functions here are the
/// small C-style API that the rest of the client uses to work with that data
/// without knowing its layout: the window manager's gadget factories call
/// them while building a gadget, GameWindow forwards some generic calls to
/// them when the window carries the matching style bit, and screen code
/// (the control bar, load screens, Apt screens) calls them directly.
///
/// Each gadget family follows the same pattern:
/// - an \e input callback that receives mouse and keyboard messages, and a
///   \e system callback that receives create, destroy, resize and gadget
///   messages (GadgetTabControlInput(), GadgetTabControlSystem(),
///   GadgetProgressBarSystem());
/// - setters that change the gadget's user data or its colours and fonts;
/// - drawing, which lives elsewhere, in the W3D device layer
///   (W3DGadgetTabControlDraw and its siblings), so nothing here draws.
///
/// In BFME 2 the retail callers of the input and system callbacks are the
/// virtual input and system methods of per-gadget GameWindow subclasses
/// (the tab control's class is the one whose draw method calls
/// W3DGadgetTabControlDraw). Zero Hour instead stored the callbacks as
/// function pointers on a plain GameWindow. The subclass reading comes from
/// the forwarding bodies in the W3D gadget sources; the class names are not
/// yet known.
///
/// All the code here is Zero Hour's GadgetTabControl.cpp, GadgetComboBox.cpp,
/// GadgetPushButton.cpp, GadgetProgressBar.cpp and GadgetTextEntry.cpp,
/// mostly reached through the BFME 1 reconstruction
/// (GadgetComboBoxSetColors() was ported directly from Zero Hour's
/// GadgetComboBox.cpp), with the BFME 2 differences noted on each function.
/// GameWindow and the window manager are still being reconstructed, so
/// they are described here only by what these functions need from them.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - None of these functions touches game logic. They change client-side
///   window state only, so changing them cannot desynchronise a multiplayer
///   game.
/// - What a layout can set: .wnd window files carry a TABCONTROLDATA block
///   and a COMBOBOXDATA block. BFME 2's .wnd loader is only partly
///   reconstructed, but its parsers for these two blocks are matched, and
///   retail's keyword table pairs each name with its parser. Each parser
///   reads its values in a fixed order and skips the label in front of each
///   value, so only the order counts. The roles of the values come from
///   Zero Hour and BFME 1: tab orientation, tab edge, tab width, tab height,
///   tab count, pane border, then a disabled-pane count followed by one flag
///   per pane; and for a combo box, editable, maximum characters, rows
///   shown, then two flags (ASCII only, letters and numbers only), which
///   BFME 2 stores as bits instead of Zero Hour's two separate fields. The
///   disabled-pane count is not checked against the eight panes, so a
///   larger count writes past the flag array. BFME 2's copy of
///   parseChildWindows has no matched row yet, and which BFME 2 screens
///   still load .wnd layouts is not established here. Zero Hour also has a
///   CHECK_LIKE window status for push buttons.
/// - Command buttons: ControlBar::setControlCommand makes a command button
///   check-like when the CommandButton's options include the flag Zero Hour
///   calls CHECK_LIKE (GadgetButtonEnableCheckLike()). That part is data.
/// - Fixed in code: a tab control has at most eight panes; the progress bar
///   takes whole percentages from 0 to 100; a text entry field accepts at
///   most 256 characters (GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetTextEntry clamps the
///   limit when the field is created); the default combo box colours
///   are hard-coded in GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook; the online
///   login screen's nickname and e-mail length limits are constants in
///   AptOnlineLogin::InitGadgets.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - These helpers make no Win32, Direct3D or audio calls. A new renderer
///   replaces the W3D gadget draw functions and the window manager's device
///   layer; this logic can stay as it is. In BFME 2 the per-gadget window
///   classes that call these input and system callbacks are defined in the
///   W3D device sources alongside the draw slots, so a replacement device
///   layer must keep that forwarding as well as replace the drawing.
/// - Geometry is in whole pixels in the gadget's own coordinate space and
///   nothing here scales it. Resolution or UI scaling has to happen when the
///   window is created or resized; a tab control recomputes its tab strip
///   and panes when it receives the resize message.
/// - GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize() is written partly in 32-bit x86
///   inline assembly to reproduce retail's instruction choice. A port to
///   another compiler or to 64-bit should use Zero Hour's plain C++ for that
///   function (GeneralsMD GadgetTabControl.cpp), whose results its
///   Implementation bullets describe.
/// - The progress bar keeps its percentage in the window's user-data
///   pointer, cast from an integer.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt


/// \file Gadget/GadgetTabControl.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief The tab control gadget: tab strip layout, pane creation and
///        sizing, and its input and system callbacks.
///
/// A tab control is a window with a strip of equal-sized tabs along one
/// edge and up to eight child pane windows filling the rest. Clicking a tab
/// shows that tab's pane and hides the others. The gadget's user data (Zero
/// Hour's TabControlData) holds the layout chosen in the editor (which edge
/// the tabs sit on, how they are aligned along it, the tab size, the tab
/// count, the border around the panes, the panes' window pointers and a
/// per-pane disabled flag) and some working values: the active tab and the
/// tab strip's rectangle. The window owns that data and frees it when it is
/// destroyed (GadgetTabControlSystem()).
///
/// Lifecycle, from the BFME 2 call graph: the window manager's tab-control
/// factory computes the tab strip (GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion()),
/// creates the panes (GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes()) and shows the first
/// one (GadgetTabControlShowSubPane()). A resize recomputes both. Zero Hour:
/// when a tab control is loaded from a .wnd file, its children are the
/// panes, and GadgetTabControlFixupSubPaneList() puts them back into the
/// pane array once they have all been created.
///
/// The bodies are Zero Hour's apart from the pane creation call and the
/// codegen shaping noted on GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize() and
/// GadgetTabControlShowSubPane().


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion(GameWindow *tabControl)
/// \brief Recomputes the rectangle occupied by the tab strip from the tab
///        control's layout settings and current size.
///
/// The result, four limits in the tab control's own coordinates, is what
/// GadgetTabControlInput() hit-tests clicks against. The tab-control factory
/// calls this once the user data is in place, and GadgetTabControlSystem()
/// calls it again after a resize.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The strip is the tab count times one tab's width (tabs on the top or
///   bottom edge) or height (tabs on the left or right edge).
/// - The strip sits inside the pane border, against the chosen edge.
/// - Along that edge it is aligned by the orientation setting: at the start
///   (top-left), centred in the free length, or at the end (bottom-right).
///   The free length is the window's length minus twice the border minus
///   the strip. Centring uses integer division, and a strip longer than the
///   window gives a negative offset; nothing is clamped.
/// - An unknown orientation leaves the offset at zero; an unknown edge
///   leaves the limits unchanged.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control. Its user data must be the tab control
///                   data; nothing is checked.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlInput(), GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize()


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize(GameWindow *tabControl, Int *width, Int *height, Int *x, Int *y)
/// \brief Computes the size and position that every pane of a tab control
///        should have.
///
/// The pane area is the tab control's window minus the pane border on all
/// four sides and minus the tab strip on the side the tabs are on. Both
/// GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes() and GadgetTabControlResizeSubPanes() use
/// it, so all panes always share one rectangle.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Width: window width minus twice the border, minus one tab width when
///   the tabs are on the left or right edge.
/// - Height: window height minus twice the border, minus one tab height when
///   the tabs are on the top or bottom edge.
/// - Position: the border, pushed right by one tab width for left-edge tabs
///   and down by one tab height for top-edge tabs. Bottom and right tabs
///   shrink the area without moving it.
///
/// The two size computations are written as x86 inline assembly that reads
/// the tab data through the register holding the user-data pointer, because
/// MSVC 7.1 compiled Zero Hour's C++ with different but equivalent
/// instructions from retail's. The values are the same as Zero Hour's
/// expressions, for example
/// `*height = winHeight - (2 * tabData->paneBorder) - tabData->tabHeight`.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control; its user data must be the tab control
///                   data.
/// \param width      [out] Pane width in pixels.
/// \param height     [out] Pane height in pixels.
/// \param x          [out] Pane left edge, relative to the tab control.
/// \param y          [out] Pane top edge, relative to the tab control.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The inline assembly only compiles for 32-bit x86 with MSVC-style
/// assembler blocks, and it depends on the compiler keeping the user-data
/// pointer in a particular register. When the build no longer needs to
/// match retail bytes, replace the two assembly blocks with Zero Hour's
/// plain C++ for the width and height (GeneralsMD GadgetTabControl.cpp),
/// whose results the Implementation bullets above describe.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion()


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetTabControlInput(GameWindow *tabControl, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of the tab control: switches panes when a tab is
///        clicked.
///
/// Only a left-button press is acted on. The mouse position arrives in
/// screen coordinates packed into mData1 and is converted to the tab
/// control's own coordinates, then tested against the tab strip that
/// GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion() stored.
/// - Outside the strip, the press is swallowed (reported as handled), so a
///   click on the tab control's background does not fall through to windows
///   behind it. (Zero Hour's comment here notes that a press on a button
///   inside a pane goes to that button and never reaches the tab control.)
/// - Inside the strip, the tab index is the distance along the strip divided
///   by one tab's size. If that pane is not disabled and is not already the
///   active one, GadgetTabControlShowSubPane() switches to it.
///
/// In BFME 2 the only retail caller is the input method of the tab
/// control's window class (see the group description).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The press-on-a-tab case has no break: it falls into the default case
/// and returns MSG_IGNORED after switching panes, exactly as Zero Hour
/// does. A press exactly on the strip's far edge gives an index equal to
/// the tab count; GadgetTabControlShowSubPane() clamps it back. A tab size
/// of zero would divide by zero.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control.
/// \param msg        Window message; only the left-button-down message is
///                   handled.
/// \param mData1     Packed mouse position in screen coordinates: x in the
///                   low 16 bits, y in the high 16 bits.
/// \param mData2     Not used.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for a press outside the tab strip; MSG_IGNORED for
///         everything else, including a press on a tab.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlSystem(), GadgetTabControlShowSubPane()


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetTabControlSystem(GameWindow *tabControl, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of the tab control: frees its data, keeps the
///        layout in step with resizes, and passes button presses up.
///
/// Messages handled:
/// - Create: nothing to do.
/// - Destroy: deletes the tab control data. The window owns it.
/// - Resized (the gadget resize message): resizes and moves every pane with
///   GadgetTabControlResizeSubPanes(), then recomputes the tab strip with
///   GadgetTabControlComputeTabRegion().
/// - Button selected: forwarded unchanged to the tab control's parent with
///   the window manager's winSendSystemMsg, and that result is returned.
///   Zero Hour creates panes with PassSelectedButtonsToParentSystem as
///   their system callback (BFME 2 stores the same function in the pane's
///   creation record), so a button pressed inside a pane reaches the pane,
///   then the tab control, then the screen that owns the tab control.
///
/// In BFME 2 the only retail caller is the system method of the tab
/// control's window class (see the group description).
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control.
/// \param msg        System message.
/// \param mData1     First message argument; passed on for a button
///                   selection.
/// \param mData2     Second message argument; passed on for a button
///                   selection.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for create, destroy and resize; the parent's result
///         for a forwarded button selection; MSG_IGNORED otherwise,
///         including a button selection when the tab control has no parent.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlInput(), GadgetTabControlResizeSubPanes()


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlShowSubPane(GameWindow *tabControl, Int whichPane)
/// \brief Makes one pane of a tab control the visible, active pane and
///        hides all the others.
///
/// Called when a tab is clicked (GadgetTabControlInput()), after the panes
/// are created (GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes(), with the current active
/// tab) and by the tab-control factory. No message is sent to anyone; the
/// active tab is simply recorded in the tab control data.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Every existing pane of the eight is hidden.
/// - The requested pane becomes active if that slot holds a window;
///   otherwise pane 0 does.
/// - The choice is then clamped to the last tab (tab count minus one), so a
///   pane beyond the tab count is never shown.
/// - The chosen pane is shown.
///
/// BFME 2 writes the clamp as a pointer select instead of Zero Hour's `min`
/// call, to match retail's code; the effect is the same.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control.
/// \param whichPane  Index of the pane to show. It is used as an array
///                   index before any check, so it must be below eight.
///
/// \warning The final pane is shown without a null check. A tab count of
///          zero, or an empty slot 0 when the requested slot is empty, makes
///          it use an invalid window pointer.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlInput(), GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes()


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes(GameWindow *tabControl)
/// \brief Creates a blank pane window for every empty pane slot of a tab
///        control and fits the existing panes to the pane area.
///
/// The tab-control factory calls it once the tab control data is in place.
/// All eight slots get a window whatever the tab count; panes beyond the
/// count simply stay hidden, because GadgetTabControlShowSubPane() never
/// picks them.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For an empty slot, a new window is created through the window manager
/// from a creation record (see the note below). It is then given the
/// tab-pane style, the name
/// "Pane" followed by its index, and the tab control's enabled state. A slot
/// that already has a window instead has its size and position set to the
/// pane area from GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize(). Finally the active
/// tab is shown with GadgetTabControlShowSubPane().
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control; its user data must be the tab control
///                   data.
///
/// \note BFME 2 creates the pane through the window manager's create call
///       that takes a creation record, not Zero Hour's winCreate with
///       explicit arguments. Retail stores five values in the record: the
///       tab control, a zero, the two pane coordinates and the address of
///       PassSelectedButtonsToParentSystem. Their roles (parent, status,
///       position, system callback) are inferred from Zero Hour's winCreate
///       arguments, not named by retail; the same first field holds an id in
///       other callers. The fields that would hold the width and height stay
///       zero, where Zero Hour passed the computed pane size. What size the
///       window manager then gives a new pane has not been traced; the next
///       resize (GadgetTabControlResizeSubPanes()) sets it to the pane area.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize(), GadgetTabControlFixupSubPaneList()


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlResizeSubPanes(GameWindow *tabControl)
/// \brief Sets every existing pane of a tab control to the current pane
///        area.
///
/// GadgetTabControlSystem() calls it when the tab control has been resized,
/// before recomputing the tab strip. Empty slots are skipped; nothing is
/// created.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control; its user data must be the tab control
///                   data.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlComputeSubPaneSize(), GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes()


/// \fn void GadgetTabControlFixupSubPaneList(GameWindow *tabControl)
/// \brief Refills a tab control's pane array from its child windows, in
///        creation order.
///
/// The function walks to the last child in the tab control's child list and
/// then back towards the first, storing each child in the next pane slot
/// from 0 upwards. Zero Hour's window manager normally puts a new child at
/// the front of its parent's list, so this reversal makes the pane array
/// follow the order in which the panes were created.
///
/// Zero Hour: the .wnd loader calls this when it finishes a TABCONTROL
/// window, once all its children exist. BFME 2: no named retail caller has
/// been identified yet; the BFME 2 copy of parseChildWindows that would call
/// it is not yet matched.
///
/// \param tabControl The tab control; its user data must be the tab control
///                   data.
///
/// \warning Every child is stored and nothing limits the count, so a tab
///          control with more than eight children writes past the pane
///          array.
///
/// \see GadgetTabControlCreateSubPanes()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief Combo box helpers; documented here: colours, text colours and
///        the text length limit.
///
/// A combo box is a window with three child gadgets: an edit box (a text
/// entry field) showing the current text, a drop-down button and a list box
/// that opens below it. Its user data (Zero Hour's ComboBoxData) points at
/// all three. BFME 2 lays that block out differently: the entry count and
/// the three child pointers sit one 4-byte slot later than in Zero Hour, so
/// some bodies here read the children by position instead of by Zero Hour's
/// field names.
///
/// This file holds only some of the combo box functions; others live in
/// their own files (GadgetComboBoxAddEntry.cpp, GadgetComboBoxGetText.cpp
/// and more).


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetColors(GameWindow *comboBox, Color enabledColor, Color enabledBorderColor, Color enabledSelectedItemColor, Color enabledSelectedItemBorderColor, Color disabledColor, Color disabledBorderColor, Color disabledSelectedItemColor, Color disabledSelectedItemBorderColor, Color hiliteColor, Color hiliteBorderColor, Color hiliteSelectedItemColor, Color hiliteSelectedItemBorderColor)
/// \brief Sets a combo box's twelve state colours on the combo box and on
///        all three of its child gadgets.
///
/// The colours come in three states (enabled, disabled, highlighted), each
/// with a fill colour, a border colour, a selected-item colour and a
/// selected-item border colour. GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook
/// is the retail caller: when a combo box gets the default look it passes a
/// fixed palette (mostly reds and yellow when enabled, greys when disabled
/// and greens when highlighted, with white for some selected-item colours)
/// before assigning the named images.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The combo box window: for each state, draw-data entry 0 gets the fill
///   and border colours and entry 1 the selected-item pair.
/// - The edit box and then the drop-down button get the same twelve
///   settings, when present.
/// - The list box, when present, gets all twelve through
///   GadgetListBoxSetColors, which spreads them over the list box and its
///   own children.
///
/// \param comboBox                        The combo box; must not be null.
/// \param enabledColor                    Fill colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledBorderColor              Border colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledSelectedItemColor        Selected-item colour when enabled.
/// \param enabledSelectedItemBorderColor  Selected-item border when enabled.
/// \param disabledColor                   Fill colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledBorderColor             Border colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledSelectedItemColor       Selected-item colour when disabled.
/// \param disabledSelectedItemBorderColor Selected-item border when disabled.
/// \param hiliteColor                     Fill colour when highlighted.
/// \param hiliteBorderColor               Border colour when highlighted.
/// \param hiliteSelectedItemColor         Selected-item colour when
///                                        highlighted.
/// \param hiliteSelectedItemBorderColor   Selected-item border when
///                                        highlighted.
///
/// \note The source fetches the children through accessor functions whose
///       current names follow Zero Hour's field order, not BFME 2's; the
///       body's local variable names give the actual roles.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The default palette is hard-coded in
/// GameWindowManager::assignDefaultGadgetLook, so restyling default combo
/// boxes means changing code; the images it assigns are looked up by name
/// (ListBoxEnabled and similar) and can be replaced in data.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors(GameWindow *comboBox, int color, int borderColor)
/// \brief Passes a combo box's enabled text colours on to its list box and
///        edit box.
///
/// GameWindow::winSetEnabledTextColors stores the colours on the window
/// itself and then, when the window has the combo box style, calls this so
/// that the text inside the combo box's children matches. The drop-down
/// button is left alone.
///
/// \param comboBox    The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param color       Text colour.
/// \param borderColor Text outline colour.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetDisabledTextColors(), GadgetComboBoxSetHiliteTextColors(),
///      GadgetComboBoxSetIMECompositeTextColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetDisabledTextColors(GameWindow *comboBox, int color, int borderColor)
/// \brief Passes a combo box's disabled text colours on to its list box and
///        edit box.
///
/// The disabled-state counterpart of GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors(),
/// called from GameWindow::winSetDisabledTextColors.
///
/// \param comboBox    The combo box.
/// \param color       Text colour.
/// \param borderColor Text outline colour.
///
/// \warning Unlike its three siblings, this function reads the combo box's
///          user data before testing the window pointer for null, so the
///          null test cannot protect it. Zero Hour has the same order and
///          retail keeps it. Its only retail caller passes its own window,
///          which is never null.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetHiliteTextColors(GameWindow *comboBox, int color, int borderColor)
/// \brief Passes a combo box's highlighted text colours on to its list box
///        and edit box.
///
/// The highlighted-state counterpart of
/// GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors(), called from
/// GameWindow::winSetHiliteTextColors.
///
/// \param comboBox    The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param color       Text colour.
/// \param borderColor Text outline colour.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetIMECompositeTextColors(GameWindow *comboBox, int color, int borderColor)
/// \brief Passes a combo box's IME composition text colours on to its list
///        box and edit box.
///
/// These are the colours for text still being composed in an input method
/// editor (used for East Asian text input). Called from
/// GameWindow::winSetIMECompositeTextColors in the same way as
/// GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors().
///
/// \param comboBox    The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param color       Text colour.
/// \param borderColor Text outline colour.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors()


/// \fn void GadgetComboBoxSetMaxChars(GameWindow *comboBox, Int maxChars)
/// \brief Sets the longest text a combo box accepts, in characters.
///
/// The limit is stored twice: in the combo box data, and as the maximum
/// text length of the edit box. Once the combo box has been built, its
/// entry-data pointer is the edit box's own data, so the second store
/// changes how much the user can type. Retail callers:
/// - the combo box factory (GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox), which
///   applies the limit from the combo box's creation data;
/// - AptOnlineLogin::InitGadgets, which limits the online login screen's
///   nickname box to 15 characters and its e-mail box to 16.
///
/// \param comboBox The combo box. Null is ignored.
/// \param maxChars Maximum number of characters. The edit box keeps it as a
///                 16-bit value.
///
/// \note The text entry factory (GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetTextEntry)
///       clamps a field's limit to 256 characters when the field is
///       created; this function does not repeat that clamp, so a larger
///       value set later is not clamped here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The online login limits above are constants in code, not data.
///
/// \see GadgetComboBoxSetColors()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetPushButton.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief Push button helpers: the check-like (toggle) mode, plus the
///        GadgetButtonGetData accessor (not documented here).
///
/// A check-like push button stays pressed when clicked and releases on the
/// next click, like a check box drawn as a button. Two pieces of window
/// state express it: a window status bit makes the button check-like, and
/// the "selected" bit of the window's instance state records whether it is
/// currently pressed. Most of Zero Hour's other push button functions are
/// in separate BFME 2 files (GadgetPushButtonInput.cpp,
/// GadgetPushButtonSystem.cpp, GadgetButtonSetText.cpp and others); a few
/// have not been located in retail yet.


/// \fn void GadgetButtonEnableCheckLike(GameWindow *g, Bool makeCheckLike, Bool initiallyChecked)
/// \brief Turns a push button's check-like (toggle) mode on or off and sets
///        whether it starts pressed.
///
/// The retail caller is ControlBar::setControlCommand, which binds a
/// command button definition to a control bar button. It turns the mode on
/// when the command button's options include the check-like flag and off
/// otherwise, and always starts the button unpressed.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sets or clears the check-like window status bit with winSetStatus or
/// winClearStatus, then sets or clears the selected bit of the window's
/// instance state. Returns early if the window or its instance data is
/// null.
///
/// \param g                The push button.
/// \param makeCheckLike    true to make the button check-like, false to make
///                         it an ordinary button.
/// \param initiallyChecked true to start pressed.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Which command buttons toggle is data: an option flag on the
/// CommandButton definition. BFME 2 tests the same bit as Zero Hour's
/// CHECK_LIKE option; the BFME 2 INI spelling of that option has not been
/// checked here. The control bar resets the pressed state each time it
/// binds the button.
///
/// \see GadgetCheckLikeButtonIsChecked(), ControlBar::setControlCommand


/// \fn Bool GadgetCheckLikeButtonIsChecked(GameWindow *button)
/// \brief Reports whether a check-like push button is currently pressed.
///
/// Reads the selected bit of the window's instance state, the bit that
/// GadgetButtonEnableCheckLike() initialises. No named retail caller has
/// been identified yet.
///
/// \param button The push button. Null gives false.
/// \return true if the selected bit is set; false if it is clear or the
///         window or its instance data is null. The window's check-like
///         status is not tested.
///
/// \see GadgetButtonEnableCheckLike()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetProgressBar.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief The progress bar gadget: its system callback and its setter.
///
/// A progress bar stores its current value, a whole percentage, directly in
/// the window's user-data pointer instead of in a separate data block.
/// Setting it is done by message: GadgetProgressBarSetProgress() sends the
/// set-progress system message and GadgetProgressBarSystem() stores it.
/// Zero Hour: the W3D progress bar draw function reads the percentage back
/// from the user data.


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetProgressBarSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of the progress bar: stores a new progress value.
///
/// Handles one message, set-progress, whose first argument is the new value.
/// A value from 0 to 100 is stored as the window's user data; anything else
/// is dropped, but the message still counts as handled. In BFME 2 the retail
/// caller is the system method of the progress bar's window class (see the
/// group description).
///
/// \param window The progress bar.
/// \param msg    System message.
/// \param mData1 For set-progress, the new percentage.
/// \param mData2 Not used.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for set-progress, valid or not; MSG_IGNORED for
///         every other message.
///
/// \note The set-progress message has a different number in BFME 2 than
///       Zero Hour's GPM_SET_PROGRESS: it is three higher, so BFME 2's
///       gadget message list differs from Zero Hour's somewhere before it.
///       Code that sends gadget messages by number cannot take Zero Hour's
///       values on trust.
///
/// \see GadgetProgressBarSetProgress()


/// \fn void GadgetProgressBarSetProgress(GameWindow *g, Int progress)
/// \brief Sets a progress bar to a percentage.
///
/// Sends the set-progress system message to the bar through the window
/// manager's winSendSystemMsg, so the value goes through
/// GadgetProgressBarSystem() and its range check. Retail callers include
/// the shell's game load screen (ShellGameLoadScreen::update), the map
/// transfer load screen and the control bar's timer display
/// (ControlBar::updateOCLTimerTextDisplay).
///
/// \param g        The progress bar. Null is ignored.
/// \param progress New value, 0 to 100. Values outside that range leave the
///                 bar unchanged.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The range is fixed at whole percentages from 0 to 100; callers convert
/// their own progress to that scale.
///
/// \see GadgetProgressBarSystem()


/// \file Gadget/GadgetTextEntry.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief Text entry helpers: GadgetTextEntrySetFont(), plus the retail
///        copy of the inline GadgetTextEntrySetTextColor().
///
/// A text entry field keeps its text in display strings held by its user
/// data (Zero Hour's EntryData): the visible text and a second string that
/// holds the masked form for secret text such as passwords. Those strings
/// need the window's font too, which is why the field has its own font
/// setter. The input and system callbacks and the character validation are
/// in other files.


/// \fn void GadgetTextEntrySetFont(GameWindow *g, GameFont *font)
/// \brief Applies a font to every display string of a text entry field.
///
/// GameWindow::winSetFont stores the font on the window and then, for a
/// window with the entry field style, calls this instead of updating only
/// the window's own strings.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Sets the font on the window's text and tooltip display strings, then on
/// the entry's text string and its masked secret-text string, skipping any
/// that does not exist. The entry strings are skipped entirely when the
/// window has no user data yet. The display strings' font setter is called
/// through its virtual table slot.
///
/// \param g    The text entry field; must not be null.
/// \param font Font to apply.
///
/// \see GadgetTextEntrySetTextColor()


/// \file GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Include/GameClient/GadgetTextEntry.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_gui_gadgets
/// \brief Zero Hour's text entry header, listed for one inline function
///        that BFME 2 retail carries as an out-of-line copy.
///
/// BFME 2's GadgetTextEntry.cpp compiles against this header, read-only in
/// the BFME 1 reference checkout. This page is listed for
/// GadgetTextEntrySetTextColor(). GadgetTextEntrySetFont() also appears
/// because the header declares it; its body is in GadgetTextEntry.cpp.


/// \fn void GadgetTextEntrySetTextColor(GameWindow *g, Color color)
/// \brief Sets a text entry field's text colour, with a darker shade for
///        its disabled state.
///
/// The enabled text colour becomes the given colour and the disabled text
/// colour a 25 percent darker shade of it; both keep the window's current
/// enabled text outline colour. It goes through
/// GameWindow::winSetEnabledTextColors and winSetDisabledTextColors, so the
/// window's own colours change.
///
/// Zero Hour defines it inline in this header and uses it when a combo box
/// updates its edit box. BFME 2 retail has a separate copy of the function,
/// which GadgetTextEntry.cpp reproduces from the same inline definition;
/// no retail caller of that copy has been identified yet.
///
/// \param g     The text entry field; must not be null.
/// \param color New text colour.
///
/// \see GadgetTextEntrySetFont(), GadgetComboBoxSetEnabledTextColors()
